   "audit_notes": {
    "claim_file_sha256": "a39d35517157ee5d83edd78fc2762e3378919c8fd9c2159f70bb2c463cd3628a",
    "claim_physical_record_sha256": "8ea142b11e565910440230396660faf9e3e652cd519ca9c34efc2b8140fffc9e",
    "claim_reasons": [
     "Terminated by statute on June 14, 1935. The sole operative command approves the Code of Fair Competition for the Wine Industry under title I of the National Industrial Recovery Act ('do hereby approve said Code of Fair Competition for the Wine Industry.'); the introductory recital and the five numbered findings command nothing. Actor: Congress, S.J. Res. 113, section 2, approved June 14, 1935. Legal basis for the first end. An approval of a code under section 3 of title I of the National Industrial Recovery Act had legal effect only through section 3: under 3(b) the code's provisions became the standards of fair competition for the trade or industry and a violation in or affecting interstate or foreign commerce was an unfair method of competition under the Federal Trade Commission Act, under 3(c) the district courts could restrain violations and district attorneys were directed to sue, and under 3(f) each violation was a misdemeanor (the section 3 excerpt held in this packet). Section 2 of S.J. Res. 113, approved June 14, 1935 (49 Stat. 375), repealed 'All the provisions of Title I of such Act delegating power to the President to approve or prescribe codes of fair competition and providing for the enforcement of such codes' and states no effective date other than its approval. That repeal removed the power the approval rested on and every provision through which the approved code was enforced, which is the reason (not a default date) that it ends the prospective operative effect of this command on June 14, 1935. Schechter Poultry Corp. v. United States, 295 U.S. 495 (decided May 27, 1935), held the section 3 code-making delegation unconstitutional ('we hold the code provisions here in question to be invalid'); the held excerpts record that as a validity characterization (an approval under an unconstitutional delegation was void from issuance under the doctrine then prevailing, and May 27, 1935 is when that was declared), not as an end date, because the judgment cancelled no other approving order and a later doctrine treats a measure's earlier existence as an operative fact. June 16, 1935 is not an end: section 1 of the resolution replaced the two-year expiry in section 2(c) with April 1, 1936, and nothing in the resolution defers section 2. No held record shows this code cancelled or suspended before June 14, 1935; EO 6900 and EO 7020 amend the code and are later events, not ends."
    ],
    "later_events_empty_verified": true,
    "review_note": "Reasoning expressly describes amendments/later events while the matching clause later_events is empty. This exactly supports the source-backed-event omission warning; it does not independently settle every event legal effect or whole lifecycle.",
    "source_examination": [
     {
      "instrument_id": "EO:7020",
      "text_path": "jobs/app-executive-orders/output/texts/executive-order-7020-amending-code-fair-competition-for-the-wine-industry.txt",
      "text_sha256": "26466d12d15896a87d7171ff47b2fc3362c292446035ebac28549c83c06dbd79",
      "span": [
       0,
       837
      ],
      "source_excerpt": "Whereas the Federal Alcohol Control Administration has submitted for my approval amendments to the Code of Fair Competition for the Wine Industry and has rendered its report and recommendations and findings thereon; and\n\nWhereas due notice of public hearing on said proposed amendments has been given and a public hearing thereon has been held pursuant to such notice;\n\nNow, Therefore, by virtue of and pursuant to the authority vested in me by Title I of the National Industrial Recovery Act of June 16, 1933 (ch. 90, 48 Stat. 195), and in order to effectuate the purposes of said Title, I hereby approve said amendments and modify my previous approval of the Code of Fair Competition for the Wine Industry to include an approval of said Code in its entirety as hereby amended.\n\nFRANKLIN D. ROOSEVELT\n\nThe White House,\n\nApril 19, 1935.\n",
      "note": "Independently read Wine Industry code amendment. EO6900 actor text was not located in this bounded audit; no external legal-effect conclusion on6900 is made."
     }
    ]
